/figure>
<p>To find the number of active intervals at any point in time, we need to =
identify when an interval has started and when an interval has ended. The s=
tart point of an interval indicates the start of a new active interval, whe=
reas an end point represents an active interval finishing. This suggests an=
 approach which looks at start and end points individually could be useful.=
 Let=E2=80=99s explore this idea further.</p>
<p><strong>Processing start points and end points</strong><br>
<!-- -->Let=E2=80=99s step through each point in the array of intervals in =
chronological order, and see if we can determine the number of active inter=
vals at each point.</p>
<hr>
<p>The first point is a start point, indicating the start of the first acti=
ve interval. So, let=E2=80=99s increment our counter:</p>
